---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/alternatives/smsbump-alternative';
const title = "SMSBump alternatives after Yotpo SMS retirement";
const desc = "Plan a replacement for retired Yotpo SMS with separate permission, number, reply, workflow and billing handovers.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I still use the SMSBump dashboard?",
    "answer": "Yotpo\u2019s lifecycle notice says the SMS dashboard and background services are inactive. Ask the provider about any remaining recovery assistance; do not assume an export screen is available."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are Reviews and Loyalty retired too?",
    "answer": "The SMS notice does not retire Reviews or Loyalty. Check each retained product and agreement separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I port my old number?",
    "answer": "Obtain confirmation for the actual number, ownership, provider and destination. Transfer eligibility and timing are not guaranteed."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an email tool replace SMSBump?",
    "answer": "Only the email portion of a separately defined project. SMS needs its own onboarding, permission, number and reply handling."
  },
  {
    "question": "What if permission evidence is missing?",
    "answer": "Quarantine those records and resolve eligibility with the receiving provider and appropriate review. Importability or an old purchase does not prove SMS permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "What establishes readiness?",
    "answer": "Verified permission, routing, replies, opt-outs, suppression and workflow tests, plus an owner for pending work and billing. A fixed migration calendar is insufficient."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify retention decisions</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">SMSBump is the legacy Yotpo SMS service. Its retirement changes the job from optimizing a live SMS module to establishing what records, numbers and responsibilities can still be recovered and transferred. Do not plan around an available legacy dashboard or an internal upgrade to the retired messaging product.</p>
      </header>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Current service status and scope</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo’s lifecycle notice</a> says the SMS application is decommissioned and its dashboard and background services are inactive. This is the operative constraint for SMSBump planning. Historical instructions to log in, export, keep legacy automations running or negotiate a renewed SMS service are not a current operating plan.</p>
<p>The notice concerns SMS messaging. It does not retire Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty, and it does not resolve every email agreement, invoice or account obligation. Identify the account owner, contracting entity and products actually used. Confirm remaining export assistance, number ownership and billing questions with the responsible provider rather than assuming a working dashboard or a universal termination date.</p>

<h2>Select a replacement by the job it must do</h2>
<p>Compare SMS-capable candidates such as Postscript, Attentive, Omnisend or Klaviyo against the same recipient countries, number types, two-way support, consent evidence, trigger and suppression tests. Obtain written onboarding requirements and current quotes for the actual traffic. A specialist and a combined email/SMS platform are both possible choices; neither is inherently the best fit.</p>
<p>Email is a separate scope decision. Keep the existing email sender if it works; evaluate an email platform such as Sequenzy or native Shopify Messaging only for a documented email requirement. Demonstrate any required Shopify integration before purchase. Adding an email app does not replace SMS number handling, carrier onboarding, replies or opt-out processing. Do not choose retired Yotpo messaging as a consolidation destination.</p>

<h2>Recover evidence without inventing access</h2>
<p>Inventory existing exports, contracts, invoices, campaign archives, integration logs and support correspondence. Ask the former provider what can still be supplied and record unavailable fields explicitly. Do not represent missing delivery history or consent as a successful export. Secure recovered files and give a named owner responsibility for access and retention.</p>
<p>Keep a stable customer identity map, normalized phone numbers, country, channel and purpose of permission, source, timestamp, disclosure version where available, and withdrawal history. Preserve exclusions even when someone previously purchased. Email permission does not establish SMS permission. Quarantine ambiguous records for review under applicable requirements and receiving-provider policy; do not send a promotional re-permission text merely because a phone number exists.</p>

<h2>Number transfer is a separate workstream</h2>
<p>Identify each sending number, type, registered owner, carrier or provider, authorization contact and intended destination. Obtain written confirmation of transfer eligibility, required documentation, fees, registration and expected sequence from the parties handling it. A number may be unavailable to port; establish an approved new-number plan and customer communication route if needed.</p>
<p>Do not promise a two-to-four-week port. With a retired service, verify what continuity remains possible rather than instructing operators to keep legacy sends active. Avoid cancelling any remaining number-related arrangement before the responsible parties confirm the consequences. Archive completion evidence, inbound and outbound routing tests and the final number inventory before treating transfer as finished.</p>

<h2>Replies, opt-outs and helpdesk handover</h2>
<p>A completed outbound test does not establish two-way continuity. Assign an inbox owner and test an ordinary reply, help request, opt-out and a reply to a message sent before transition. Determine where legacy replies can still arrive, whether any history can be recovered, and how open conversations will be handled when it cannot. Give support a documented fallback contact route.</p>
<p>Test the receiving provider’s opt-out behavior and suppression propagation into other sending tools before promotion. Reconcile late withdrawals and failed imports. If several numbers or systems are involved, document the scope of each suppression and prevent a second system from marketing to a withdrawn contact. Registration and platform acceptance do not replace review of the permission evidence.</p>

<h2>Apparel-brand rehearsal with Reviews and Loyalty retained</h2>
<p>This is a proposed fixture, not a merchant success report. Model an apparel store with cart SMS, flash-sale campaigns, review requests and loyalty-tier notifications. Record which messages used the retired channel and which still have a working sender. Retain Reviews and Loyalty where useful; do not remove their widgets, points ledger or integrations as collateral to a messaging change.</p>
<p>Test a cart followed by a purchase during the delay: the replacement must recheck order state before dispatch. Replay the same event and require one enrollment. Test a withdrawn SMS contact who still has email permission, an email-only subscriber with a phone number, an unavailable product variant and a refunded order. Each must have an explicit expected send, skip or fallback result.</p>
<p>Map review requests, loyalty events, subscription events and helpdesk replies to an owner and disposition: rebuild, retain with another sender, retire deliberately or hold until evidence is available. A points update must not silently disappear because cart SMS passed. Campaign and recovery queues need independent reconciliation. Preserve email and SMS permissions separately across any shared customer profile.</p>

<h2>Activate by evidence gates</h2>
<p>First establish recoverable records and the accepted permission population. Next settle number and reply routing, authenticate any email sender being changed and test forms, preference links and checkout capture. Then rebuild one approved workflow with purchase suppression, quiet-hour handling and event idempotency. Verify eligibility again at dispatch, including current permission and order state.</p>
<p>Assign one active sending queue per trigger across email and SMS. Coordinate timing and collision rules intentionally rather than requiring every email to stop whenever a text is sent. Reconcile pending work and historical events before enabling enrollment; an imported event should not trigger a stale recovery message. A rollback may mean pausing the replacement, not reactivating a retired application.</p>
<p>Expand only after agreed tests and monitoring pass. Record delivery failures, complaints, opt-outs, support response and duplicate incidents. Do not schedule a peak-sale launch around an unconfirmed port date. Calendar targets can organize work but do not establish readiness.</p>

<h2>Quote the whole service and close the right accounts</h2>
<p>Compare destination-country mix, message segments, encoding, media, carrier charges, number fees, registration, support and minimum commitments. Include email separately if changing it, plus rebuild and staff time. Remove generic legacy price tables: a retired SMS product is not an available quote or a continuing benchmark for renewal negotiation.</p>
<p>Illustrative budget only: $250 monthly fee savings minus three hours of extra work valued at $50 leaves $100 recurring benefit. A $1,500 handover cost takes fifteen months to recover on those assumptions. Five extra hours removes that benefit. Do not count all attributed recovery revenue as incremental margin; use comparable cohorts and a holdout where feasible.</p>
<p>Finance and the authorized account owner must reconcile credits, final invoices, retained contracts and number arrangements independently. Get written closure for the intended messaging scope. Keep Reviews and Loyalty billing and access intact when retained. Archive migration evidence and expire contractor access according to the agreed handover, not an arbitrary universal retention period.</p>
      </article>
      <nav class="mt-12 max-w-4xl" aria-label="Related guides">
        <p>Related reading: these comparisons provide context; verify current supplier scope before choosing.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives</a></p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/alternatives/postscript-alternative">postscript alternative</a></p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/alternatives/yotpo-email-sms-alternative">yotpo email sms alternative</a></p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/apps/smsbump">smsbump</a></p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump">postscript vs smsbump</a></p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">consent compliance</a></p>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
